      "editorial": {
        "en": {
          "why_it_matters": "The interesting thing here is not simply Paquito playing Chick Corea. The project places a musician who has spent his career moving freely among Afro-Cuban music, bebop, chamber writing, and classical technique inside Emilio Solla's orchestral reworking of Corea's Latin-facing repertoire. Because the symphonic language already exists on Ritmo, the live question becomes how D’Rivera's clarinet and alto voice can cut through and reshape that architecture in real time rather than merely decorate it.",
          "trip_verdict": "Actively make room for one of the two Rose Theater nights if a New York trip is already in play. The U.S. debut, D’Rivera, Solla, and full symphonic frame make it substantially stronger than a routine tribute, while the two-night run and existence of the underlying recorded project keep it below the level where the whole trip should be rebuilt around this event alone.",
          "listen_before": "Josep Vicent, Emilio Solla & ADDA Simfònica — The Chick Corea Symphony Tribute. Ritmo"
        },
